  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math> -ANOMER OF MALTOSE</p><p>maltose is disaccharides of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math> -<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>D</mi></math>- glucopyranose by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> glycosidic linkage</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/608887ee-90c4-436a-8bff-2a218f083d25-image.png\" style=\"width: 285px; height: 116px;\" /></p><p>Maltose or malt sugar is a disaccharide composed of two glucose molecules connected with an alpha (1,4) glycosidic bond.</p><p>Maltose is a reducing sugar. It&#39;s two glucose molecules must be linked in such a way as to leave one anomeric carbon that can open to form an aldehyde group.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
